1
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:12,080
Right? The place is small,
but it's cheap and really good.

2
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:16,460
Still, senpai,
you really know all kinds of places in Ueno.

3
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:20,200
I came to Ueno to play all
the time when I was a kid.

4
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,720
You can still see the
traces of that little fire.

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,440
What are you talking about?

6
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:30,640
When we were kids,
we nearly burned Benten-dō to the ground.

7
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:35,300
We held a bicycle race around Shinobazu Pond,
or something like that.

8
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:39,340
But we crashed, and everyone ended
up jumping into Shinobazu Pond.

9
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:44,660
Lotus leaves tangled around the bikes and our bodies,
and it was a real struggle to crawl out.

10
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:47,240
On top of that,
it was December and freezing cold.

11
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:50,220
We built a bonfire beside
Benten-dō to dry ourselves off.

12
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:53,620
Then, as luck would have it,
there was a kerosene can right nearby.

13
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:55,700
Did you set it on a bonfire?

14
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:57,140
Exactly.

15
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:01,440
It suddenly flared up, and the fire
spread all the way to the Benten Hall.

16
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:05,000
I desperately scooped up pond
water with my shoes to put it out.

17
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,320
So if you'd made one wrong move,
that Benten Hall would've been gone?

18
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:10,760
That's right.

19
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:15,780
Your old stories are always so
dynamic and chilling, Senpai.

20
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:18,900
Right, we've come all this way nearby.

21
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,280
Let's stop by my place.

22
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:28,140
When you watch TV, turn on the lights
in the room and sit back from the screen.

23
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:41,260
Anyone without money, come to my place.

24
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:45,980
I don't have any either, so don't worry.

25
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:49,820
Look at that blue sky.

26
00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:53,060
White clouds.

27
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:01,580
Three thousand miles in
search of Nakagawa's knight

28
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:11,800
Old man

29
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:13,020
Kankichi

30
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:16,420
Pilfering the shop's
takings for pachinko again

31
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:17,840
Shut up.

32
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:20,060
Don't just show up out of nowhere like that.

33
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:22,140
Long time no see.

34
00:03:23,060 --> 00:03:26,460
Why, it's Mr. Nakagawa! Ayame!

35
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:30,760
What's this? Kankichi, why are you here?

36
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:39,080
Mr. Nakagawa, hello! I heard a car stop,
so I came out, and it was this guy.

37
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:48,940
Don't lie! Why would you take the shop's takings when you're leaving the house?
Was I? I've been so forgetful lately, it's terrible.

38
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:52,620
You were just planning to go lose at pachinko again,
weren't you?

39
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,580
Come on, give it back.

40
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:57,260
Doesn't seem like Mom, though.

41
00:03:57,260 --> 00:03:59,240
Well said, Kankichi!

42
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,120
That's exactly what a
good-for-nothing son is.

43
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:04,500
Crying won't help you now.

44
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:07,260
Who's the idiot here?

45
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:12,380
Kankichi, won't you have some tea before you go,
Nakagawa-san?

46
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,900
It's not like we've got
anything much to offer anyway.

47
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:17,620
Don't feel obligated, all right?

48
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:19,980
Ha-ha!

49
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:23,560
Damn it, my big win just went up in smoke!

50
00:04:23,560 --> 00:04:26,680
Big talk coming from you!
What kind of tip was this?

51
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:31,300
Betting on crap long shots like this is
just like throwing your money down the drain!

52
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:34,300
With a crappy tip like that,
I should kill you!

53
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:37,320
You've really done it now,
you stupid old man!

54
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:41,520
Senpai, you really are hopeless.

55
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,620
Nakagawa, hold this for a second.

56
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:44,080
Okay.

57
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:48,380
Let's put an end to this.

58
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:50,220
Damn it!

59
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:52,460
That's enough.

60
00:04:52,460 --> 00:04:55,140
The shoji was only replaced last month.

61
00:04:55,140 --> 00:04:56,940
What a hopeless pair of mother and son.

62
00:04:57,820 --> 00:04:58,440
Here you go.

63
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:01,580
Honestly, both of you, at your age...

64
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:04,340
Don't go playing around like a kid.

65
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:07,100
I'm not playing around, okay?

66
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:09,960
Look, over there.

67
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:12,800
What's with bringing out an album?

68
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:14,940
For Kankichi's wife.

69
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:17,860
She said she wanted to see
photos of Kankichi as a kid.

70
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:27,020
But I couldn't find them for ages. Silver lining Yeah,
right. I'm the one who got hurt.

71
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:31,680
So there are photos of you as a kid too, Senpai?
Yeah, there are.

72
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:39,160
This one's from when Kankichi entered a national junior high,
taken at a photo studio.

73
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:46,380
Kankichi was like the star, Mera-chan...
he's always been like this, Nakagawa-san.

74
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:49,880
He hasn't changed at all from now.

75
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:51,540
You're saying strange things.

76
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:54,640
Back then, Dad was drinking like crazy.

77
00:05:55,380 --> 00:05:56,480
His face was red, right?

78
00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:58,020
Yeah, it was.

79
00:06:00,580 --> 00:06:03,040
That was when you won the
boys' baseball tournament, right?

80
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:05,600
Your father was the coach, then?

81
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:06,880
Well, yeah.

82
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:10,600
That win was practically thanks to my leadership,
you know.

83
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:12,000
Don't make me laugh.

84
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,660
On the day of the final, our team's
manager collapsed with a high fever.

85
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:20,840
So I had no choice but to have Dad take over,
that's all.

86
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:21,900
I see.

87
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:27,940
Now that I think about it, you were drinking on the bench
back then too and got warned by the umpire, you idiot old man.

88
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:31,100
How dare you call your
own father an idiot old man!

89
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:34,060
What, you want to go again,
you idiot old man?

90
00:06:34,060 --> 00:06:36,420
I've told you both to stop!

91
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:36,920
Stop it.

92
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:40,380
I envy you.

93
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,140
What do you mean by that, Nakagawa?

94
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:48,260
I don't have a single
photo taken with my father.

95
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:50,040
Not even one?

96
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:55,280
No. I've met my father maybe
ten times since I was born.

97
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:59,520
Nakagawa's old man's motto is
"Can you work 72 hours straight?"

98
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:02,900
He's the world's busiest super businessman,
after all.

99
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:19,620
I'd have to have my ultra-packed
schedule adjusted just to see my father,

100
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:22,380
and even when we do meet, he's always sick.

101
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:25,180
Even a rich young master has it tough

102
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:30,680
I envy you, Senpai, and you and your father who
can say whatever you want to each other like this.

103
00:07:31,580 --> 00:07:33,640
What? You're meeting your dad?

104
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:36,660
My father's secretary called me just now,

105
00:07:36,660 --> 00:07:42,280
saying my father wants to meet me
and hand over something important.

106
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:45,880
That world's busiest father
went out of his way for that?

107
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:46,480
Yes.

108
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:48,674
I wonder what it could be.

109
00:07:48,724 --> 00:07:50,560
This important thing...

110
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:51,400
Well...

111
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:55,240
But this way we can take a two-shot photo with Dad,
can't we?

112
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,620
Alright, I'll take it for you.

113
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:01,880
So where are you meeting, then?

114
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:04,580
Well, it's a bit far.

115
00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:05,760
New York?

116
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:08,100
No, it's even farther.

117
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:09,780
Then Brazil?

118
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:11,540
No, even farther.

119
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:13,940
Brazil is on the opposite side of Japan,
you know.

120
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:16,800
There's no country farther than that.

121
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:20,620
It's not a country, we're meeting in space.

122
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:22,700
What?

123
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:25,380
You're meeting in space?

124
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:29,540
Hey, hey, only people from
Tokyo would get that place name.

125
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:32,340
It's famous because it has a racetrack.

126
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:34,120
I don't know, I don't know.

127
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:35,840
Where in space are you meeting?

128
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:37,740
At the space station.

129
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:42,860
What, a space station?
You're meeting in front of the station?

130
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:45,700
That's why you shouldn't use local jokes.

131
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:50,920
My father is building a space station so that
space travel can begin commercial operations in 2003.

132
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:53,180
He's building the station, you know.

133
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:56,820
So we agreed to meet at the site.

134
00:08:56,820 --> 00:09:01,320
What used to be manga and
sci-fi is becoming reality now

135
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:08,260
Yes, it's about two years behind 2001: A Space Odyssey,
the sci-fi film.

136
00:09:08,260 --> 00:09:14,380
Back in grade school, the
"2001: A Space Odyssey" gag was a huge hit.

137
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:16,520
Quit pestering me, Ryotsu!

138
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:18,200
So when are you going?

139
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:19,280
Tomorrow.

140
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:23,900
Nakagawa Space Development has a
launch base on its artificial island.

141
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:28,320
That's where they'll launch from,
using the crewed rocket.

142
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:35,740
Right now the station is passing over Japan,

143
00:09:35,740 --> 00:09:39,780
so we'll dock a rocket
with it and meet up there.

144
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:40,920
Dad.

145
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:41,800
Keichi.

146
00:09:41,800 --> 00:09:45,540
That's quite a big-scale father-son reunion.

147
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:48,000
That's going to cost a fortune.

148
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:51,000
Well, it's reasonably so.

149
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:54,740
Yes, this is me.

150
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:57,960
The launch has been cancelled.

151
00:09:57,960 --> 00:09:59,340
What?

152
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:02,380
Wait a moment.

153
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,320
Reiko, sorry,
could I have a world map, please?

154
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:13,540
Latitude and longitude... is it here?

155
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:18,500
Got it, thanks.

156
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:20,240
What's going on?

157
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:23,500
Apparently my father has
something urgent to attend to.

158
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:26,240
He's apparently heading back
to Earth in a capsule rocket.

159
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:30,380
So the spot you marked on that
map is the splashdown point?

160
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:34,620
Yes, the meeting place was changed to there.

161
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:37,920
Off New Guinea? That's quite a change.

162
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:39,240
What?

163
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:42,160
I think it's a helicopter to pick us up.

164
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:52,560
Well then, Chief,
I'd like to take paid leave starting today.

165
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:53,420
Mm.

166
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:57,400
Nakagawa,
I'll take a two-shot with your dad for you.

167
00:10:57,400 --> 00:10:59,120
Please do.

168
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:01,000
Then I'll take paid leave too.

169
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,500
You used up all of yours long ago.

170
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:06,180
Any days off will be
deducted from your salary.

171
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:06,960
Chief.

172
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:09,180
Senpai, please hurry.

173
00:11:09,180 --> 00:11:10,060
Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

174
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:18,480
Ken-chan,
I hope you get to meet your father safely.

175
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:26,380
Looks like we made it in time.

176
00:11:26,380 --> 00:11:26,944
Yes,

177
00:11:26,994 --> 00:11:30,680
splashdown is scheduled in
12 minutes and 18 seconds.

178
00:11:30,680 --> 00:11:33,160
Who would have thought my dad
would come falling from the sky.

179
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:34,760
I remember now.

180
00:11:35,840 --> 00:11:37,940
When I was still in elementary school,

181
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:42,360
I once rode in a private
plane with my father.

182
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:48,440
but we ran into a storm over the Matterhorn.

183
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:53,260
Keiichi,
don't make such a fuss over this much.

184
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:54,260
Y-yes, sir!

185
00:11:54,260 --> 00:11:56,600
Don't you trust your father's skill?

186
00:11:58,980 --> 00:11:59,940
I do trust it.

187
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:01,800
Good boy, Keiichi.

188
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:05,200
In the 21st century,
there's something I want to give you.

189
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:10,860
We're breaking through the thunderclouds!

190
00:12:10,860 --> 00:12:12,560
Hold on tight.

191
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:35,820
Once we broke through above the clouds,
the storm from earlier seemed like a lie.

192
00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:38,160
and a peaceful world spread out before us.

193
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:41,120
Dad, about what you said earlier...

194
00:12:41,740 --> 00:12:44,660
Father went quiet again and kept piloting.

195
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:51,760
I think the thing Dad wants to give me
is probably what he mentioned back then.

196
00:12:53,420 --> 00:12:55,240
A father and son being
this dramatic is too much!

197
00:12:55,240 --> 00:12:58,400
Normally,
when a father and son have a talk like that,

198
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:02,500
it's on a riverbank at sunset
or after playing catch, right?

199
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:05,400
In our family, this is normal.

200
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:07,360
Keiichi-sama, the capsule is here.

201
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:11,920
Nakagawa, you're finally going to
get a two-shot photo with your old man!

202
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:12,560
Yes.

203
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:15,360
It's completely burnt black.

204
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:16,080
What?

205
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:19,400
It looks like it spent
too long in the atmosphere.

206
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:20,340
This is bad!

207
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:22,120
P-please, go ahead.

208
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:37,300
The inside may also be extremely hot.

209
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:38,580
Open it carefully.

210
00:13:38,580 --> 00:13:39,220
Copy that.

211
00:13:40,540 --> 00:13:41,240
It's open.

212
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:43,000
They're not here.

213
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:44,400
No one is on board.

214
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:45,300
What?

215
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:47,300
How did they escape?

216
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:49,260
I have no idea.

217
00:13:52,020 --> 00:13:52,460
Yes.

218
00:13:53,620 --> 00:13:55,800
They're returning to Earth by shuttle?

219
00:13:56,300 --> 00:13:56,940
What?

220
00:13:57,420 --> 00:13:59,820
It will arrive at the Miami Space Center.

221
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:00,680
Understood.

222
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:02,400
Another change?

223
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:04,540
I'll call a helicopter right away.

224
00:14:04,540 --> 00:14:05,980
Yes, do that.

225
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:12,240
W-what?!

226
00:14:13,060 --> 00:14:15,700
About an hour ago, he came back
to get something he'd forgotten.

227
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:17,760
He went back to his home, alone.

228
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:19,780
What a busy father he is!

229
00:14:19,780 --> 00:14:21,700
So where is this home?

230
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:26,740
He has homes in 72 places around the world,
starting with Tokyo.

231
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:28,300
72 places?

232
00:14:28,620 --> 00:14:31,160
Anyway, where's the closest one?

233
00:14:31,820 --> 00:14:33,420
Let's see... maybe Florida?

234
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:35,560
Alright, let's go there first.

235
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:38,580
What? Did you find out which home it is?

236
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:43,640
We just got word that he's
heading to his home in New York.

237
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:46,020
Alright, hey, New York it is!

238
00:14:46,540 --> 00:14:46,860
Yes!

239
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:57,160
Having an airfield and a golf course
right in the middle of New York?

240
00:14:57,160 --> 00:14:59,760
What on earth is your father up to?

241
00:15:03,780 --> 00:15:05,300
So where is Dad?

242
00:15:05,300 --> 00:15:08,480
He said he's on private time from now on.

243
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:11,700
without telling the company
or us where he was going,

244
00:15:11,700 --> 00:15:13,860
and then he just went out.

245
00:15:14,260 --> 00:15:16,640
Damn, we were so close.

246
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:18,300
No leads again.

247
00:15:18,780 --> 00:15:21,720
Senpai, forget it. I'm giving up.

248
00:15:23,420 --> 00:15:30,480
What are you talking about? If you give up now,
you'll never get a photo with your dad!

249
00:15:31,820 --> 00:15:41,400
You're right, I understand. Let's put a bounty on my father.
After all, he's always lived his life by the second.

250
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:52,460
If we don't catch him by force, I may never see him again.
Yeah, true. Put a full-page newspaper ad saying we'll pay $20 million to whoever finds him.

251
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:54,300
$20 million?

252
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:55,920
Understood.

253
00:15:56,940 --> 00:15:59,600
At 120 yen to the dollar,

254
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:01,760
so, so, so, so, so...

255
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:03,260
2.4 billion yen!

256
00:16:03,380 --> 00:16:03,780
Again!

257
00:16:04,820 --> 00:16:05,420
Whoa!

258
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:07,520
I'll find him myself, no matter what!

259
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:10,720
So I'm taking all the reward money! Got it?

260
00:16:10,900 --> 00:16:11,520
Y-yes.

261
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:15,000
What? You'll pay 400 million yen?

262
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:16,660
I'm in on that deal!

263
00:16:17,140 --> 00:16:21,600
Send me a photo of that President
Nakagawa right away by email!

264
00:16:23,420 --> 00:16:26,160
Hey, Nakagawa.
You have a photo of your father, don't you?

265
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:27,460
Yes, here it is.

266
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:29,340
Hold on a second.

267
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:30,520
Okay.

268
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:34,820
Then send that photo to
this address right away.

269
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:35,460
Yes.

270
00:16:36,260 --> 00:16:37,480
Then I'm counting on you for this.

271
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:39,260
Understood.

272
00:16:41,140 --> 00:16:43,740
Good, Colonel Bagryu is taken care of now.

273
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:47,190
Nakagawa, from here on,
use the full resources of the Nakagawa Group

274
00:16:47,240 --> 00:16:50,300
and search all of America,
no, the entire world.

275
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:50,740
Yes.

276
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:58,440
Army, navy, air force,
use everything you've got to find him.

277
00:16:58,440 --> 00:17:00,140
Incoming message from Colonel McClure.

278
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:00,760
What?

279
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:04,480
They just took him into
custody in San Francisco.

280
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:05,940
Right, let's go!

281
00:17:11,420 --> 00:17:13,140
Finally, I get to see Dad.

282
00:17:17,900 --> 00:17:18,840
Here he comes.

283
00:17:39,720 --> 00:17:40,360
Dad

284
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:42,560
Sorry to interrupt your work.

285
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:44,580
This was the only way I could think of.

286
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:46,440
No.

287
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:46,920
No!

288
00:17:48,340 --> 00:17:49,560
That's not Dad!

289
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:50,680
What?

290
00:17:52,380 --> 00:17:55,700
He looks similar, but he's a different person.
Look at the photo carefully.

291
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:59,200
Even so, all I can make out is his profile.

292
00:18:01,240 --> 00:18:02,180
It's me!

293
00:18:03,060 --> 00:18:05,380
What? You found him? Where?

294
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:08,860
This is the search team.
To confirm, we're getting closer,

295
00:18:08,910 --> 00:18:11,320
and we'll film footage and send it to you.

296
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:18,940
I can't see his face.
Zoom in closer on the image.

297
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:19,260
Yes.

298
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:21,640
Did you hear that?
Give instructions right away.

299
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:26,720
There's no doubt about it.

300
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:28,340
Okay, go, Kaida!

301
00:18:43,700 --> 00:18:44,820
Dad

302
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:50,440
Half his face is totally different!

303
00:18:51,380 --> 00:18:53,140
No way.

304
00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:55,020
Nakagawa!

305
00:18:55,020 --> 00:18:58,140
The trip wore me out, I can't go on.

306
00:18:58,140 --> 00:18:59,900
Pull yourself together, Nakagawa!

307
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:01,800
We've found another one.

308
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:03,360
This one too.

309
00:19:17,240 --> 00:19:18,380
Good work.

310
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:20,780
There's 2.4 billion yen riding on this!

311
00:19:20,780 --> 00:19:23,660
We need Nakagawa to take a proper look.

312
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:24,460
Yes.

313
00:19:24,460 --> 00:19:27,200
So far we've located these 12 men.

314
00:19:29,040 --> 00:19:30,760
Nakagawa, take a look at this!

315
00:19:36,180 --> 00:19:37,060
Dad

316
00:19:37,060 --> 00:19:40,440
Dads everywhere!

317
00:19:44,220 --> 00:19:49,380
Dads everywhere!

318
00:19:49,380 --> 00:19:53,340
There are tons of Dads here!

319
00:19:53,340 --> 00:19:56,340
Dads everywhere!

320
00:19:56,340 --> 00:19:58,560
Get a grip, Nakagawa!

321
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:01,200
For now, let's check every location!

322
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:03,560
Get a fighter jet ready, ASAP!

323
00:20:03,560 --> 00:20:08,140
It's in England!

324
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:10,740
Is he the real deal?

325
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:12,920
The eyes are different.

326
00:20:15,620 --> 00:20:17,480
How about Switzerland?

327
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:20,480
The nose is completely different.

328
00:20:24,020 --> 00:20:25,200
Egypt!

329
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:27,560
His chin's way too cleft.

330
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:33,080
What about Brazil?

331
00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:36,000
Nobody has this many moles.

332
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:37,440
Eeeeeh!

333
00:20:40,080 --> 00:20:44,220
Come on, what about China? Your head...

334
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:54,140
I'm losing my mind! It's hopeless, so don't you dare fall asleep!
Don't you want to see Nakagawa's old man?

335
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:59,820
Dad must've become a star. Look, over there.

336
00:21:02,500 --> 00:21:04,700
What are you babbling about?

337
00:21:04,700 --> 00:21:06,280
Don't give up on this!

338
00:21:06,280 --> 00:21:09,380
If we don't find him,
the 2.4 billion goes poof.

339
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:12,320
Dad.

340
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:14,240
Where, where, where is he?

341
00:21:15,120 --> 00:21:20,200
When I close my eyes like this, look,
Dad's right there behind my eyelids.

342
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:23,220
If you're going to say that, it should be
"Mother behind the eyelids," shouldn't it?

343
00:21:23,720 --> 00:21:25,920
Mo-Mom... Dad's smiling!

344
00:21:26,820 --> 00:21:28,080
Pull yourself together!

345
00:21:29,820 --> 00:21:30,500
Nakagawa!

346
00:21:32,740 --> 00:21:35,200
May I go now?

347
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:38,880
Ryotsu, Nakagawa.

348
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:42,080
Even after mobilizing the entire globe,
we couldn't find him.

349
00:21:42,700 --> 00:21:44,520
We don't have a single lead.

350
00:21:44,520 --> 00:21:46,400
Kei-chan, you're a wreck too.

351
00:21:46,920 --> 00:21:48,200
We've given up on the reward.

352
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:50,300
We're making it a public search.

353
00:21:50,840 --> 00:21:52,680
It's Kei-chan's cell phone!

354
00:21:56,140 --> 00:21:57,840
It's an email from your father.

355
00:21:57,840 --> 00:21:58,840
What!?

356
00:21:59,820 --> 00:22:01,560
He says he's waiting in front of Sensō-ji.

357
00:22:01,560 --> 00:22:04,040
No way! He's in Japan?

358
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:10,480
No way, he couldn't be somewhere like this!

359
00:22:11,100 --> 00:22:13,560
Found him! He's really here!

360
00:22:13,820 --> 00:22:15,120
Nakagawa, wake up!

361
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:15,960
It's my father!

362
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:21,780
Dad! Dad!

363
00:22:24,106 --> 00:22:26,800
Keiichi! Keiichi!

364
00:22:27,800 --> 00:22:29,920
Dad!

365
00:22:31,820 --> 00:22:33,840
Good for you, Nakagawa.

366
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:36,300
Thank you, senpai.

367
00:22:36,300 --> 00:22:38,620
Keiichi, there's no time.

368
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:39,400
Yes.

369
00:22:39,400 --> 00:22:41,860
I have something to give you.

370
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:43,320
Yes.

371
00:22:44,300 --> 00:22:47,300
I meant to give it to you last year,
when the new century began,

372
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:49,760
but it's become this year.

373
00:22:50,300 --> 00:22:50,820
Yes.

374
00:22:52,680 --> 00:22:56,000
That's it! The toy in a jar!

375
00:22:59,000 --> 00:22:59,700
Uuu...

376
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:06,900
That's the one you get by collecting one gold octopus or five
silver octopuses from the octopus balls and sending them in,

377
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:08,980
isn't it just a toy in a jar?

378
00:23:08,980 --> 00:23:13,740
When I asked Keiichi, before he started elementary school,
what he wanted for the future,

379
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:17,160
he said he wanted this,
so I entered and won it for him.

380
00:23:17,160 --> 00:23:21,920
I've kept it safe all this time,
and today I finally got to give it to you.

381
00:23:22,980 --> 00:23:25,140
President, it's time.

382
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:25,780
Yeah.

383
00:23:36,100 --> 00:23:38,700
Well then, let's meet again in a few years.

384
00:23:38,700 --> 00:23:40,580
Farewell, Keiji.

385
00:23:41,180 --> 00:23:43,780
Your father is really a busy man

386
00:23:43,780 --> 00:23:47,020
Kei-chan, Kei-chan, pull yourself together!

387
00:23:49,780 --> 00:23:50,500
I'm exhausted.

388
00:23:51,720 --> 00:23:55,280
But I'm really glad I got
to take a photo with Dad.

389
00:24:20,020 --> 00:24:27,440
I probably don't want to be the kind
of guy who plays dumb games over looks.

390
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:34,380
While I was dawdling at the corner,
the opening slipped away.

391
00:24:34,380 --> 00:24:42,600
Now isn't everything,
so don't get so worked up.

392
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:46,340
Even if you cheat a little with a lie.

393
00:24:46,340 --> 00:24:50,500
Sometimes while helping someone.

394
00:24:50,500 --> 00:24:52,500
Baby, do you know me?

395
00:24:52,560 --> 00:24:56,900
A nice spirit heading toward tomorrow.

396
00:24:58,060 --> 00:25:01,940
Don't rot away,
I'll do what needs to be done.

397
00:25:01,940 --> 00:25:04,320
A cute spirit.

398
00:25:04,320 --> 00:25:07,720
The hard-to-understand part is

399
00:25:08,880 --> 00:25:11,320
Trying to make it look that way,

400
00:25:13,260 --> 00:25:17,420
isn't that better than
just redoing the look?

401
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:24,460
Na-na-na-na-na-na...

402
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:31,200
I'm starving.

403
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:33,380
I want to eat breakfast already.

404
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:35,740
Why is it always dogs, old ladies,

405
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:37,860
purse snatchers,
and competitive eating champions?

406
00:25:37,860 --> 00:25:40,240
Everyone keeps getting in my way.

407
00:25:40,240 --> 00:25:43,140
Just let me eat my food!

408
00:25:43,680 --> 00:25:47,720
Next time, catch Ryo-san's long day, okay?

409
00:25:56,020 --> 00:25:56,540
Thanks.

